From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: close two teardown races the success path never walks

Two independent defects, one theme: a path that tears hardware down while
something else is still using it. Neither is a regression, neither is on the
HW-proven success path, and neither has been observed on silicon -- they are
read out of the code and out of the locking rules the two subsystems already
state for themselves. Found by code review of
the display driver.

Line numbers below are the files as they stand after `drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle`, i.e. the tree this
patch applies to.

FIX 1 -- sun60i_dp_aux_transfer() checks dp->enabled with no barrier at all
===========================================================================

WHAT IS WRONG

sun60i-dp.c:453 reads dp->enabled and then, twelve lines later, does MMIO
against the block that flag describes. It is the only shared field in
struct sun60i_dp read outside dp->lock: link_lanes, link_rate, dpcd_valid,
hpd_asserted and enabled itself are held under it in .detect (:3043),
.edid_read (:3069), .hpd_notify (:3106), .atomic_enable (:3178),
.atomic_disable (:3244), .attach (:3292), .detach (:3345), teardown (:3382)
and all nine debugfs verbs. Here there is nothing.

sun60i_dp_aux_disable() (:1365) runs on the other side of that read:
phy_power_off(), phy_exit(), reset_control_assert(), clk_disable_unprepare().

THE SCENARIO, AND THE DOOR IT COMES THROUGH IS NOT THE ONE THE COMMENT NAMES

The guard's own comment blames /dev/drm_dp_auxN. That node cannot exist in this
image: it is gated on CONFIG_DRM_DISPLAY_DP_AUX_CHARDEV
(drivers/gpu/drm/display/Kconfig:35), which configs/a733_defconfig does not
carry, so drm_dp_aux_register_devnode() is the stub at
drm_dp_helper_internal.h:23. ⚠ The symbol was RENAMED upstream from
DRM_DP_AUX_CHARDEV, so grepping the old spelling returns nothing and looks like
confirmation -- it is not evidence either way, and the review that found this
defect cited the old name.

The real door is CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV=y (configs/a733_defconfig:429). Once
.attach has called drm_dp_aux_register() the I2C-over-AUX adapter is published
permanently as /dev/i2c-N, and dp->lock appears nowhere between an
i2c_transfer() there and this function.

So: a process holds /dev/i2c-N open and issues a transfer. The Type-C cable is
pulled. typec_displayport calls drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event(), which reaches
sun60i_dp_bridge_hpd_notify(), which takes dp->lock and calls
sun60i_dp_aux_disable(). Best case the reader has passed :453 and is inside
readl_poll_timeout(), so it polls a dead window for its full 3 ms and reports
-ETIMEDOUT instead of -ENODEV -- which drm_dp_dpcd_access() then retries 32
times, exactly what the guard exists to prevent. Worst case a readl()/writel()
lands after clk_disable_unprepare(), which is MMIO against a block whose bus
clock is off. This driver has never measured that state; `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` measured only the
gated-plus-reset one, where the window reads zero.

⚠ THE OBVIOUS FIX IS A DEADLOCK, AND IT IS NOT CLOSE

The review proposes taking dp->lock in sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(), on the
statement that "no caller holds it except .edid_read, which then needs a
_locked variant". That statement is false, and it is false nine times over.
Every one of these holds dp->lock and then issues AUX through a drm_dp_*
helper, which lands in this function:

  sun60i_dp_video_write()      :1324 guard -> sun60i_dp_video_sink_confirms_stream()
                                     -> drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status()  :1070
  sun60i_dp_link_write()       :2512 guard -> sun60i_dp_link_configure() :1618
  sun60i_dp_train_show()       :2596 guard -> drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status() :2601
  sun60i_dp_train_write()      :2670 guard -> sun60i_dp_train()  (the whole ladder)
  sun60i_dp_dpcd_show()        :2699 guard -> drm_dp_dpcd_read_data()    :2704
  sun60i_dp_edid_show()        :2774 guard -> sun60i_dp_edid_block() -> i2c
  .edid_read                   :3069 guard -> drm_edid_read_ddc()
  .hpd_notify                  :3106 guard -> sun60i_dp_link_probe() -> :1429
  .atomic_enable               :3178 guard -> sun60i_dp_train()  -> :1710 :1763
                                                :1848 :1850 :1932 :1934 :2048 :2120

dp->lock is a plain devm_mutex_init() mutex (:3515). Re-acquiring it in the
transfer path is an immediate self-deadlock on EVERY modeset, EVERY hot-plug
and EVERY debugfs verb -- i.e. it would hang precisely the path that is
hardware-proven. It is not a matter of adding one _locked variant; it is nine
call chains, several of them recursive through the training ladder.

WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD

Nothing at all in sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(). Not one lock, not one extra load on
the hot path; the dp->enabled test stays exactly as the hardware proof left it.

The barrier goes one level up, onto the lock that every route to aux->transfer
ALREADY holds and that this driver was simply not using: aux->hw_mutex.

  * drm_dp_dpcd_access() takes it around the whole 32-retry loop
    (drm_dp_helper.c:594, unlocked at :643).
  * the i2c path takes it through the adapter's own lock ops --
    drm_dp_aux_init() installs drm_dp_i2c_lock_ops (drm_dp_helper.c:2358;
    the ops struct itself is at :2244-2248), lock_bus() is mutex_lock(&aux->hw_mutex) (:2229-2232), and
    i2c_transfer() calls __i2c_lock_bus_helper() -> i2c_lock_bus() before
    __i2c_transfer() (i2c-core-base.c:2313-2318, i2c-core.h:37-50).
  * the aux chardev would take it too, and does not exist here anyway.

The public API for exactly this is drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(), whose kerneldoc
describes the case verbatim: "If the device attached to the aux bus is powered
down then there's no reason to attempt a transfer. Error out immediately"
(drm_dp_helper.c:596-599, the check itself at :600-603). So:

  1. sun60i_dp_aux_disable() calls drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(&dp->aux, false) as
     its FIRST statement, before it touches the PHY, the reset or the clock.
     That call takes aux->hw_mutex, so it cannot return while a transfer is
     still inside sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(); and once it HAS returned,
     drm_dp_dpcd_access() (:600) and drm_dp_i2c_xfer() (:2148) both bail with
     -EBUSY without calling aux->transfer at all. The teardown below it is
     therefore unreachable while an MMIO access to the block is in flight, in
     both directions.
  2. sun60i_dp_aux_enable() opens it again, LAST, after the gate, the reset,
     the PHY and the divider are all up.
  3. probe() closes it right after drm_dp_aux_init(), because the field is
     zero-initialised to "powered" and .attach can register the DDC adapter --
     and with it /dev/i2c-N -- before any sun60i_dp_aux_enable() has run.

WHY THE SUCCESS PATH CANNOT CHANGE

This is the constraint that shaped the fix, so it is spelled out rather than
asserted:

  * Zero new locking in the transfer path. sun60i_dp_aux_transfer() is
    byte-identical apart from its comment. No AUX transaction is serialised
    against anything it was not already serialised against, because every one
    of them already ran under aux->hw_mutex before this patch.
  * No lock is held across a DRM core call. drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered() takes
    aux->hw_mutex, sets a bool and drops it; it is three of the driver's own
    lines, in enable, disable and probe, and none of them is inside a callback
    the core invokes with a lock of its own.
  * No new lock ORDER either. The order dp->lock -> aux->hw_mutex is what the
    driver has done since `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` -- every entry in the nine-call-chain table
    above is exactly that -- and these three calls follow it. The inverse
    order, aux->hw_mutex -> dp->lock, is what taking dp->lock in
    sun60i_dp_aux_transfer() would have created, and it is the reason that fix
    deadlocks. It is not created here.
  * The error code the guard was chosen for is preserved. The comment argues
    -ENODEV over -ETIMEDOUT because the core retries a timeout 32 times. The
    helper's own refusal is -EBUSY, returned BEFORE that retry loop is entered
    (drm_dp_helper.c:600-603, ahead of the retry loop at :611), so nothing is
    retried.
  * The wait in sun60i_dp_aux_disable() is bounded and sleepable. All four
    callers (:2424 debugfs aux_enable, :3132 .hpd_notify, :3352 .detach, :3384
    teardown) already hold dp->lock, so the context sleeps. One AUX transaction
    is capped at AUX_REPLY_TIMEOUT_US = 3 ms by the readl_poll_timeout() at
    :514; the helpers bound their own retries (32 in drm_dp_dpcd_access(),
    aux->ddc.retries = 3). Worst case is a few hundred milliseconds on a cable
    unplug, against MMIO into an unclocked block.

FALSIFIER

No board is needed for the deadlock half; it is a lockdep question and lockdep
answers it. Boot with CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING=y and run, in this order:

  echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
  cat    /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/dpcd     # dp->lock -> hw_mutex
  i2cdetect -y -r $(i2c-N of the DP AUX adapter)        # hw_mutex alone
  echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable

Expect: all four complete, dmesg carries no "possible circular locking
dependency" and no "held lock freed". REFUTED by any lockdep splat naming
dp->lock and hw_mutex together -- that would mean an inverse acquisition exists
somewhere this analysis missed.

For the race itself, on a board, with a monitor attached and AUX up:

  while :; do i2cget -y <N> 0x50 0x00 >/dev/null 2>&1; done &
  # unplug the Type-C cable

Expect: the reader's errors turn to -EBUSY (i2cget prints "Read failed") at the
instant `hot-plug: sink gone` appears, and dmesg carries no "AUX poll expired"
line from after it. REFUTED by an "AUX poll expired" line timestamped after
`hot-plug: sink gone`, which would mean a transfer got past the gate -- and
refuted just as firmly by an SError or an interconnect stall, which would mean
the gate closed too late.

⚠ NOT PROVEN ON SILICON. The defect was read, not measured, and so is the fix.

FIX 2 -- sun60i_de_start()'s error path resets the DE with a layer fetching
===========================================================================

WHAT IS WRONG

sun60i-de.c:1294 calls sun60i_de_layer(), which stages LAY0_ATTCTL with the
enable bit and the scanout address de->fb_dma. On the only commit backend this
driver has, .stage is sun60i_de_mmio_stage() -> sun60i_de_write() (:521-524),
a plain store -- so the write has LANDED when that call returns, not when
->arm() does. From that point the layer is armed to fetch on the TCON's next
frame-start pulse, which is the state the driver itself announces at :1314 as
"scanning ... from %pad".

The two checks that follow both jumped to err_clocks (:1298, :1309), and
err_clocks is clk_disable_unprepare() + reset_control_assert()
(sun60i_de_clocks_off()). No sun60i_de_layer_disable(), no sun60i_de_quiesce().

The regular path does both, in that order, and sun60i_de_stop() (:1337-1342)
says why in its own header: "it has to be given time to finish what is in
flight before its reset is asserted underneath it". The comment above
DE_QUIESCE_FRAMES (:350-357) is blunter: "Asserting a bus master's reset in the
middle of an AXI burst is the classic way to wedge an interconnect, and the
memory the layer was reading goes straight back to CMA immediately afterwards."

The error path was doing precisely the thing the driver documents as forbidden,
and it was reachable only from a failure -- so the first symptom would have
been a wedged interconnect during a bring-up failure, i.e. the moment when
nobody trusts any other observable either.

THE SCENARIO

sun60i_de_verify() (:759) checks two registers in order: channel VI0
LAY0_ATTCTL first, blender 0 PIPE_CTL second. The interesting failure is the
one where the FIRST passes and the second does not -- ATTCTL read back exactly
as written, so the layer is fetching, while the blender window refused the
write ("the window is open but did not take the write", :789). verify returns
-EIO, and before this patch the next thing to happen was reset_control_assert()
on a live AXI master.

On the debugfs path it gets worse by one step: sun60i_de_enable() (:1351) calls
sun60i_de_fb_free() on failure, so dma_free_coherent() hands the pages the DE
may still be reading straight back to CMA.

WHAT THIS DOES

One extra landing label. Jumps taken AFTER the first datapath write go to
err_layer:, which does sun60i_de_layer_disable() + sun60i_de_quiesce() and then
falls through to err_clocks: -- the same sequence, in the same order, that
sun60i_de_stop() uses.

sun60i_de_check_alive()'s jump (:1279) deliberately still goes straight to
err_clocks: it runs before sun60i_de_top_open(), so no datapath register has
been written, no bus master is fetching, and there is nothing to quiesce.

Two details that were checked rather than assumed:

  * sun60i_de_quiesce() divides by de->mode.clock. de->mode is assigned at
    probe (:1960, sun60i_display_mode_1920x1200) and overwritten by
    drm_mode_copy() in sun60i_de_kms_enable() (:1740) before sun60i_de_start()
    is ever called, so it is never zero on either path. This is the same
    expression sun60i_de_stop() already evaluates unconditionally.
  * the ->arm() failure at :1298 also routes to err_layer, even though on the
    MMIO backend sun60i_de_mmio_arm() cannot fail (it returns 0
    unconditionally, :526-536). If a future RCQ backend makes that reachable,
    the staged ATTCTL may never have committed and the quiesce is 34 ms wasted
    on a failure path -- which is the cheap direction to be wrong in.

COORDINATION WITH `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes` -- CHECKED AGAINST ITS TEXT, NOT GUESSED AT. `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes` landed
while this patch was being written and does make sun60i_de_layer_disable()
return int (sun60i-de.c:1107 after it). It adds no __must_check, so the
err_layer: call site here compiles clean at W=1 on the combined stack -- and it
is the same call site convention sun60i_de_stop() itself uses two lines later,
which also drops the return. If a later patch adds __must_check, both call
sites need the return consumed, not just this one.

FALSIFIER

The failure is not reachable by hand today, so the falsifier is the code path,
made visible:

  # a DE that fails verify: hold the blender window shut by starting the DE
  # from a state where de_top was never opened -- or simply instrument it
  echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5000000.display-engine/enable

Expect on a verify failure: the two dev_err lines from sun60i_de_verify(), then
a ~34 ms gap (two frames at the 1920x1200 mode), then the block goes to reset,
and `cat state` afterwards reads LAY0_ATTCTL as 0. REFUTED by LAY0_ATTCTL still
carrying its enable bit after the failure, or by no gap at all -- either means
the quiesce did not run.

The stronger falsifier is the one this patch cannot run: a failure injected
while the TCON is genuinely scanning, with the DE's AXI port watched. Nobody
has done that, and nobody should conclude from its absence that the old path
was safe -- the interconnect stall it risks is not something a verify failure
would survive to report.

⚠ NOT PROVEN ON SILICON.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES HERE
=========================

The answer differs per fix.

Fix 1: sunxi_drm_edp.c cannot have this defect, but only because it
never calls drm_dp_aux_register(), never publishes an I2C-over-AUX adapter and
hand-rolls its own AUX entry points, so there is no externally reachable caller
to race in the first place. It also has no equivalent guard at all: its
transfer path does no enabled-check, which is the defect `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` already answers.

Fix 2: the A523/T527 series carries a patch of its own titled "channel quiesce
before stopping" -- which this driver's DE_QUIESCE_FRAMES comment already cites
as the precedent. What neither that series nor the vendor does is apply the
same care to the FAILURE path. That is what this patch adds, and it is one
label.

VERIFIED
========

  applies clean : yes, BOTH WITH AND WITHOUT `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths` and `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes`, `patch -p1 --fuzz=0`
                  on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36 in each case. No .rej, no
                  .orig, either way.
                    (a) series alone -- all 129 of patches/*.patch in glob
                        order, then this patch. Every hunk at offset 0.
                    (b) the real stack -- that series, then `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths`, then `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes`,
                        then this patch. Applies with line offsets only
                        (dp.c +40/+65/+95, de.c +14), which --fuzz=0 permits:
                        it forbids fuzzy CONTEXT matching, not relocation.
                  The hunks are cut against the series-alone tree (a) deliberately,
                  because that makes the patch independent of whether `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths` and
                  `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes` are present -- (a) and (b) are both real runs, not one
                  run and one inference.
                  ⚠ Where it came CLOSE: `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths`'s `@@ -626,18 +675,9 @@` rewrites
                  sun60i_dp_aux_enable()'s adapter-add block, ~15 lines above
                  this patch's `dp->enabled = true` hunk. It does not touch the
                  tail, so the ordering this fix depends on survives -- verified
                  by reading the merged function, not by the exit status:
                  dp->enabled = true, then drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(true), then
                  return 0, with every err_ label below them.
  builds        : yes, both trees -- `make ARCH=arm64 W=1
                  drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/`, native aarch64 gcc 14.2.0, with
                  DRM_SUN60I_{DP,DE,TCON_TV,KMS}=m and I2C_CHARDEV=y. All four
                  objects compile with NO warning on the series-alone tree and
                  NO warning on the `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths`+`drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes`+`drm-a733-close-two-teardown-races` stack.
  new symbols   : one, drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(), declared at
                  include/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h:534 and exported at
                  drm_dp_helper.c:693. <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h> is already
                  included by this file (:87), so no new include.
  checkpatch    : `--strict --terse --no-signoff` reports 0 errors, 0 checks
                  and 1 warning -- the >75-char commit description, which `drm-a733-dp-detect-reports-hot-plug-not-dpcd-valid`
                  and `drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle` emit identically and which is this tree's headerless
                  patch format, not a defect in this patch.
  lockdep       : NOT run. The deadlock analysis above is a reading of every
                  dp->lock acquisition in the file plus every path the DP
                  helper takes to aux->transfer; CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING is the
                  falsifier, and nobody has booted it.
  on silicon    : NO. Neither fix. Both defects were read out of the code.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
@@ -1191,7 +1191,7 @@ static int sun60i_de_start(struct sun60i_de *de)
 	ret = de->commit->arm(de);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(de->dev, "commit (%s): %d\n", de->commit->name, ret);
-		goto err_clocks;
+		goto err_layer;
 	}
 
 	/*
@@ -1202,7 +1202,7 @@ static int sun60i_de_start(struct sun60i_de *de)
 	 */
 	ret = sun60i_de_verify(de);
 	if (ret)
-		goto err_clocks;
+		goto err_layer;
 
 	de->enabled = true;
 	de->layer_on = true;
@@ -1216,6 +1216,28 @@ static int sun60i_de_start(struct sun60i_de *de)
 
 	return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * TWO LANDING LABELS, and which one a failure takes is decided by one
+	 * question: has sun60i_de_layer() run? Everything above it is the
+	 * control plane, so a failure there leaves no bus master fetching and
+	 * err_clocks is the whole of the cleanup -- which is why
+	 * sun60i_de_check_alive() still jumps straight there.
+	 *
+	 * From sun60i_de_layer() onwards it is not. That call stages
+	 * LAY0_ATTCTL with the enable bit and the scanout address, and
+	 * .stage on the only backend this driver has is a plain
+	 * sun60i_de_write() -- so by the time either of the two checks below it
+	 * can fail, the layer is armed to fetch from de->fb_dma on the TCON's
+	 * next frame-start pulse. Dropping into err_clocks from there asserts
+	 * the DE's reset underneath a live AXI master, which is precisely what
+	 * the comment at DE_QUIESCE_FRAMES exists to forbid, and on the debugfs
+	 * path sun60i_de_enable() then hands the same pages back with
+	 * dma_free_coherent(). This is the failure the error path was creating
+	 * out of a failure the hardware had already contained.
+	 */
+err_layer:
+	sun60i_de_layer_disable(de);
+	sun60i_de_quiesce(de);
 err_clocks:
 	sun60i_de_clocks_off(de);
 
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -422,19 +422,44 @@ static ssize_t sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(struct drm_dp_aux *aux,
 	 *
 	 * Until drm_dp_aux_register() had a caller, every route into this
 	 * function ran through a debugfs verb or through an i2c adapter that
-	 * only exists while the block is enabled. drm_dp_aux_register() adds two
-	 * that do not: the /dev/drm_dp_auxN chardev, which any process holding
-	 * it open can drive at any instant, and an adapter that stays registered
-	 * across an `echo 0 > aux_enable`. Both land here with the bus clock
-	 * gated and the reset asserted -- the state in which `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` MEASURED the
-	 * whole 4 KiB window reading zero. The first readl() would then see
-	 * "no request in progress", the AUX_COMMAND write would go nowhere, and
-	 * the poll below would burn its full 3 ms before reporting a timeout
-	 * that says nothing about the sink.
+	 * only exists while the block is enabled. drm_dp_aux_register() adds one
+	 * that does not: an I2C-over-AUX adapter that stays registered across an
+	 * `echo 0 > aux_enable`, which CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV=y publishes as
+	 * /dev/i2c-N for any process to drive at any instant. It lands here with
+	 * the bus clock gated and the reset asserted -- the state in which `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`
+	 * MEASURED the whole 4 KiB window reading zero. The first readl() would
+	 * then see "no request in progress", the AUX_COMMAND write would go
+	 * nowhere, and the poll below would burn its full 3 ms before reporting a
+	 * timeout that says nothing about the sink.
+	 *
+	 * ⚠ NOT the /dev/drm_dp_auxN chardev this comment used to name. That node
+	 * is gated on CONFIG_DRM_DISPLAY_DP_AUX_CHARDEV (drm/display/Kconfig:35 --
+	 * the symbol was renamed from DRM_DP_AUX_CHARDEV upstream, so the old
+	 * spelling greps clean in this tree and proves nothing), and it appears
+	 * nowhere in configs/a733_defconfig: drm_dp_aux_register_devnode() is the
+	 * stub at drm_dp_helper_internal.h:23 and the node does not exist. What
+	 * DOES exist is CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV=y (a733_defconfig:429). Naming the
+	 * wrong door is what kept the real hole invisible: this is a PLAIN READ
+	 * of dp->enabled, and dp->lock -- which every other shared field in this
+	 * struct is held under -- is nowhere in the i2c path.
+	 *
+	 * ⚠ AND IT CANNOT BECOME THE BARRIER BY TAKING dp->lock HERE. That is a
+	 * self-deadlock, not a fix: .atomic_enable, .hpd_notify, .edid_read and
+	 * five debugfs verbs all hold dp->lock across drm_dp_* helpers that land
+	 * in this function. The barrier is one level up instead, where the lock
+	 * that every route to aux->transfer ALREADY holds lives:
+	 * sun60i_dp_aux_disable() calls drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(false), which
+	 * takes aux->hw_mutex, and after it returns drm_dp_dpcd_access() and
+	 * drm_dp_i2c_xfer() both refuse before calling us at all.
+	 *
+	 * This test survives as the second line, and as the only cover for the
+	 * window between bind and the first sun60i_dp_aux_enable().
 	 *
 	 * -ENODEV, not -ETIMEDOUT: the DRM core retries a timeout (32 times, per
 	 * drm_dp_dpcd_access()) and does not retry a missing device, and no
-	 * number of retries can change this answer.
+	 * number of retries can change this answer. The helper's own refusal is
+	 * -EBUSY, returned before that retry loop is entered, so it keeps the
+	 * property this line was chosen for.
 	 */
 	if (!dp->enabled)
 		return -ENODEV;
@@ -667,6 +692,13 @@ static int sun60i_dp_aux_enable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	}
 
 	dp->enabled = true;
+
+	/* Opened LAST, on purpose: from here a transfer that wins aux->hw_mutex
+	 * finds a block whose gate, reset, PHY and divider are all up. The
+	 * counterpart is the first line of sun60i_dp_aux_disable().
+	 */
+	drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(&dp->aux, true);
+
